"""Generate the Phase 2 Range/Cells and PivotTables Excel fixtures (task_041).

This is a committed, idempotent fixture-authoring tool (not production code). It
builds two macro-enabled workbooks through a **hidden, non-activating**
``Excel.Application`` created with ``DispatchEx`` and adds a small deterministic
VBA module through VBIDE:

* ``RangesCellsProject.xlsm``
  ``Cells`` sheet — constants (string / number / bool / blank), formulas,
  date-formatted values, Excel error cells, a 2-D block, a merged area, data
  validation (list + whole-number) and conditional formatting (cell-value +
  expression). A ListObject ``tblCells`` plus ranges **inside**, **overlapping**
  and **outside** the table so ListObject structural guards can be tested.
  ``StructOps`` sheet — copy / insert / delete / merge targets.
* ``PivotTablesProject.xlsm``
  ``Data`` and ``TableData`` local sources, and ``PivotReport`` with three
  PivotTables: two share one worksheet-backed ``PivotCache`` (``ptA``, ``ptB``)
  and a third uses a ListObject-backed cache (``ptC``). ``ptA`` carries row,
  column, page and data fields; ``ptB`` carries a manual (``PivotItems.Visible``)
  filter. Everything is local and offline: no connections, no Power Query, no
  Data Model.

The manifest written to ``tests/fixtures/phase2_manifest.json`` is a **design
oracle**: the expected cell values / formulas / number formats / validation /
conditional formatting / ListObject range / pivot layout / aggregation / filter
state are literal constants below. Every run rebuilds both workbooks in a
temporary directory, reads them back through Excel, asserts the readback matches
those constants, and only then copies them into ``tests/fixtures`` and records
the SHA-256 of each committed file. An interrupted or mismatched run therefore
never corrupts an existing fixture and never leaves temp files behind.

It requires Windows + Excel + (optionally) VBOM trust and must run serially::

    uv run python tests/fixtures/build_phase2_fixtures.py

No ``.Activate`` / ``.Select`` / ``SendKeys`` is used anywhere; the owned Excel
instance stays ``Visible = False`` for its whole lifetime (addendum BG03/BG04).
"""

